14. How can Jenkins start a build?

Common methods:
Manual
Webhook
Poll SCM
Scheduled trigger
Upstream job

1. Webhook
Webhook means GitHub tells Jenkins: "New code was pushed."

Example:

Developer
   |
   | git push
   ↓
GitHub
   |
   | "New code arrived!"
   | webhook
   ↓
Jenkins
   |
   ↓
Build starts
Example

You push code at 10:00 AM.

GitHub immediately sends a webhook to Jenkins.

Jenkins starts the pipeline:

10:00 → Developer pushes
10:00 → GitHub sends webhook
10:00 → Jenkins starts

Simple meaning:  Webhook = GitHub tells Jenkins.

2. Poll SCM

Poll SCM means Jenkins repeatedly checks GitHub to see whether new code is available.

Example:

Jenkins
   |
   | Check GitHub
   ↓
No new code

After some time...

Jenkins
   |
   | Check GitHub
   ↓
New code found!
   |
   ↓
Build starts

For example, you configure Jenkins to check every 5 minutes:

10:00 → Check GitHub → No change
10:05 → Check GitHub → No change
10:10 → Check GitHub → New code → Build

Simple meaning:

Poll SCM = Jenkins asks GitHub.

Webhook vs Poll SCM
Webhook:
GitHub → Jenkins
"Hey! New code."

Poll SCM:
Jenkins → GitHub
"Do you have new code?"

Webhook is generally preferred when available because Jenkins doesn't need to keep checking.

3. Scheduled Trigger
This means:  Run the Jenkins job at a particular time or interval, regardless of whether code changed.
Example:  You want your job to run every day at 10 PM.

10 PM
 ↓
Jenkins
 ↓
Run job

Or you could schedule something periodically.

Example:
Every day
     ↓
Jenkins job

This is useful for things like:
Nightly builds
Regular testing
Cleanup jobs
Scheduled reports

Simple meaning:  Scheduled trigger = Jenkins runs according to a clock/schedule.


4. Upstream Job
This means:  One Jenkins job finishes, and then another Jenkins job starts.


Suppose you have two jobs:
Job A = Build
Job B = Deploy

You configure Job B to start after Job A succeeds.
Job A
Build
  ↓
Success
  ↓
Job B
Deploy

If Job A fails, then normally Job B will not start when Job B is configured to run only after Job A succeeds.
Example:
Build Application
       ↓
       ↓
Test Application
       ↓
       ↓
Deploy Application

Here:

Build job = upstream
Deploy job = downstream

So:
Upstream Job
     ↓
Downstream Job
Simple meaning:  Upstream = previous Jenkins job triggers the next Jenkins job.

