job ordering in Jenkins, it means controlling the order in which Jenkins jobs/pipelines run.

For example:

Job 1: Build
      ↓
Job 2: Test
      ↓
Job 3: Deploy
In Jenkins Pipeline

You normally control the order using stages:

pipeline {
    stages {

        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                sh 'mvn package'
            }
        }

        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                sh 'mvn test'
            }
        }

        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                sh './deploy.sh'
            }
        }
    }
}

Jenkins runs them in this order:

Build
  ↓
Test
  ↓
Deploy

The next stage starts after the previous stage completes successfully.

If you have separate Jenkins jobs

You can also make one job trigger another:

Job A
Build
  ↓
Job B
Test
  ↓
Job C
Deploy

For example:  Build Job → Test Job → Deploy Job
So, job ordering = deciding which Jenkins job should run first, second, third, etc.


So the upstream/downstream relationship is one way to create job ordering.

Important difference
Job ordering is the general idea:  "Run these jobs in this order."

Upstream/downstream describes the relationship between those jobs:   "Job B is triggered by Job A, so A is upstream and B is downstream."
