                sh './deploy.sh'
            }
        }
    }
}

The important point is:

linux-agent
    ↓
mvn clean package
    ↓
JAR/WAR created

Then Jenkins moves to:

deploy-agent
    ↓
./deploy.sh
But does the JAR automatically go from one EC2 to the other?

No.

Each agent can have its own workspace.

EC2-1: linux-agent
workspace/
└── target/
    └── app.jar


EC2-2: deploy-agent
workspace/
└── ??? 

The app.jar created on linux-agent is not automatically available on deploy-agent.

You need to transfer the artifact.

A common Jenkins way is:

stage('Build') {
    agent { label 'linux-agent' }

    steps {
        sh 'mvn clean package'
        stash name: 'app', includes: 'target/*.jar'
    }
}

stage('Deploy') {
    agent { label 'deploy-agent' }

    steps {
        unstash 'app'
        sh './deploy.sh'
    }
}

Now the flow is:

linux-agent EC2
      |
      | mvn clean package
      ↓
    app.jar
      |
      | stash
      ↓
    Jenkins
      |
      | unstash
      ↓
deploy-agent EC2
      |
      ↓
  ./deploy.sh

So the correct interview answer is:

A later stage can run on a different agent, but files created on the first agent are not automatically
available there. We must transfer the artifact, for example using stash/unstash, an artifact repository, or another file-transfer mechanism.


With stash/unstash

In the Build stage:

stage('Build') {
    agent { label 'linux-agent' }

    steps {
        sh 'mvn package'
        stash name: 'app', includes: 'target/*.jar'
    }
}

After mvn package creates app.jar, this:

stash name: 'app'

takes the file from the linux-agent workspace and stores it with Jenkins.

Then Deploy stage:

stage('Deploy') {
    agent { label 'deploy-agent' }

    steps {
        unstash 'app'
        sh './deploy.sh'
    }
}

unstash 'app' takes the stored file and puts it into the deploy-agent workspace.
So:

EC2-1
linux-agent
   |
   | app.jar
   ↓
stash
   |
   ↓
Jenkins
   |
   | unstash
   ↓
EC2-2
deploy-agent
   |
   ↓
app.jar



One important point
Jenkins Controller is not necessarily the place where the build runs.
The Controller manages the pipeline. The stash data is handled by Jenkins, and unstash makes it available in the workspace of the next agent.
So you can remember:  Build agent creates the file → stash stores it → deploy agent gets it with unstash.

