In your code:

stage('Build') {           
    steps {    
        dir("java_hello") {         
            sh 'mvn package'         
        }  
    }
}
What does dir("java_hello") mean?

dir() means change the current working directory for the steps inside it.

So:
dir("java_hello") {
    sh 'mvn package'
}

means:  Go inside the java_hello directory, then run mvn package there.
Example
Suppose Jenkins workspace is:

workspace/
└── java_hello/
    ├── pom.xml
    └── src/

Normally Jenkins is here:  workspace/
But:
dir("java_hello") {
    sh 'mvn package'
}

changes the working location to:  workspace/java_hello/

Then Maven runs there:
workspace/java_hello/
        |
        └── pom.xml
              ↓
        mvn package
Why use dir()?

Usually because your project is inside a subdirectory of the Jenkins workspace.

For example:

workspace/
├── README.md
├── java_hello/
│   ├── pom.xml
│   └── src/
└── other-project/

You want Maven to use:  java_hello/pom.xml
So you write:
dir("java_hello") {
    sh 'mvn package'
}
Very important

dir() does not move the files to another EC2/agent.
It only changes the directory where the command runs.

dir("java_hello")
       ↓
change folder

stash / unstash
       ↓
transfer files between agent workspaces

So:  dir() = go into a particular folder in the current workspace.

If your workspace is:
workspace/
├── README.md
├── java_hello/
│   ├── pom.xml
│   └── src/
└── other-project/

and you run:

sh 'mvn package'

Jenkins runs it from:

workspace/

Maven looks for:  workspace/pom.xml

It doesn't automatically go into:

workspace/java_hello/pom.xml

So you need either:

Option 1 — dir()
dir('java_hello') {
    sh 'mvn package'
}
Option 2 — Tell Maven where the POM is
sh 'mvn -f java_hello/pom.xml package'
If pom.xml is directly in workspace

Then no dir() is needed:

workspace/
├── pom.xml
└── src/
sh 'mvn package'

So remember: Jenkins starts commands in the workspace root by default. It doesn't automatically search subdirectories.
