What is Jenkins Shared Library?

A Shared Library is a place where you keep common Jenkins Pipeline code so that multiple Jenkinsfiles can reuse it.
What is Jenkins Shared Library?

Imagine you have 3 projects:

Project A
Project B
Project C

All 3 Jenkins pipelines need the same Docker build code.

Without Shared Library:

Project A Jenkinsfile
    ↓
docker build ...

Project B Jenkinsfile
    ↓
docker build ...

Project C Jenkinsfile
    ↓
docker build ...

You write the same code 3 times.

With a Shared Library:

             Shared Library
             dockerBuild()
                   ↑
          ┌────────┼────────┐
          │        │        │
       Project A Project B Project C
       Jenkinsfile Jenkinsfile Jenkinsfile

You write the common code once.

Full example

Suppose your Git repository looks like this:

jenkins-shared-library
│
└── vars
    └── dockerBuild.groovy
dockerBuild.groovy
def call(String imageName) {
    sh "docker build -t ${imageName}:latest ."
}

This means:

Create a function called dockerBuild.

Now your project Jenkinsfile
@Library('my-shared-library') _

pipeline {

    agent any

    stages {

        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                dockerBuild('myapp')
            }
        }

    }
}

Look carefully:

dockerBuild('myapp')

You did not write the Docker command here.

Instead, Jenkins goes to the Shared Library:

dockerBuild('myapp')
       ↓
vars/dockerBuild.groovy
       ↓
def call(String imageName)
       ↓
docker build -t myapp:latest .

So Jenkins finally executes:

docker build -t myapp:latest .
Why is it called "Shared"?

Because many Jenkins projects can use the same library.

For example:

Shared Library
     │
     ├── dockerBuild()
     ├── dockerPush()
     ├── dockerLogin()
     └── deployToKubernetes()
            ↑
            │
    ┌───────┼────────┐
    ↓       ↓        ↓
Project A Project B Project C

If 20 projects need dockerBuild(), all 20 can use the same function.

One important point

Shared Library is NOT the Jenkinsfile.

They are separate.

Git Repository 1
└── Jenkinsfile
        ↓
        uses
        ↓
Git Repository 2
└── Jenkins Shared Library
    └── vars
        └── dockerBuild.groovy

The Jenkinsfile says what the pipeline should do.

The Shared Library contains reusable code that the Jenkinsfile can call.

Very simple interview answer

Jenkins Shared Library is a centralized place where we store reusable Jenkins pipeline code, so multiple Jenkins projects can use the same code instead of duplicating it in every Jenkinsfile.


Why do we use it?

Main purpose: Reusability + Centralized management

For example, if you change the Docker build process:

Without Shared Library:

Change 10 Jenkinsfiles manually.

With Shared Library:

Change the common code once.
Projects using that library can use the updated logic.
Where is it stored?

Usually the Shared Library code is stored in a Git repository:

Git Repository
   |
   └── Jenkins Shared Library
           |
           ├── vars/
           ├── src/
           └── resources/
Interview answer
Jenkins Shared Library is a centralized repository of reusable Pipeline code that can be shared across multiple Jenkins projects.
It helps avoid duplicate Jenkinsfile code and makes pipeline maintenance easier.
